HashMap is a collection in Java that stores data in Key-Value pairs.
Exp -----------
HashMap<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put(1, "Vivek");
map.put(2, "Rahul");
map.put(3, "Amit");

System.out.println(map);

o/p------{1=Vivek, 2=Rahul, 3=Amit}
Problem:__ HashMap is not thread-safe.
=========================================================================

ConcurrentHashMap is a thread-safe implementation of the Map interface.
It allows multiple threads to access and update the map safely.
                  Exp -------

                          ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, String> map =
                                  new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
                          
                          map.put(1, "Vivek");
                          map.put(2, "Rahul");
                          map.put(3, "Amit");
                          
                          System.out.println(map);

Multiple threads can work with this map concurrently.

======================================================================

How does ConcurrentHashMap work internally?
This is the most important interview concept.

In modern Java (Java 8 and above), ConcurrentHashMap uses:
1. CAS (Compare-And-Swap).
2. synchronized for certain bucket-level updates.
3. Volatile fields for visibility.
4. Fine-grained coordination instead of locking the entire map for every update.













